    "author": "DisposeMail Team",
    "category": "Guides",
    "image": "/blog/tempmail-xyz-dispomail-xyz-free-temporary-email-guide.svg",
    "content": "<p>If you typed \"dispomail\", \"disposalmail\", \"disposmail\" or \"dispose mail\" into a search engine and landed here, you are in the right place. This page explains what those names refer to and how to tell the real service from lookalikes.</p>\n<h2>Who we are</h2>\n<p>DisposeMail is a free disposable email service at <strong>disposemail.xyz</strong>. You open the homepage, receive a temporary address, and read incoming messages in your browser. There is no account and no registration. Inboxes and their messages are deleted automatically after one hour.</p>\n<h2>About the different spellings</h2>\n<p>The name is easy to misspell. \"Dispomail\", \"disposmail\", \"disposalmail\" and \"disposal mail\" are all variants people use when they are looking for a disposable mail service. Some of those names also belong to unrelated sites. DisposeMail is not affiliated with dispose.lol, tempmail sites on other domains, or any service that merely has a similar name.</p>\n<h2>How to check you are on the right site</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The address bar should read <strong>disposemail.xyz</strong>.</li>\n<li>The homepage should give you an address immediately, with no login or payment screen.</li>\n<li>We never ask for your real password, your card number or your identity documents.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If a site claiming to be DisposeMail asks for any of those, close it.</p>\n<h2>What you can do here</h2>\n<p>Besides the temporary inbox, we offer a <a href=\"/extension\" class=\"text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 font-semibold underline\">browser extension</a>, and a set of <a href=\"/free-tools\" class=\"text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 font-semibold underline\">free tools</a>: a password generator, a data breach checker, a Base64 encoder, a JWT decoder and more. Most run entirely in your browser.</p>\n<h2>What to use it for, and what not to</h2>\n<p>Use a temporary address to keep your real email out of marketing lists, to download something once, or to test a signup form you are building. Do not use it for accounts you will need to recover, and do not use it to get around the terms of any service. Read the <a href=\"/faq\" class=\"text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 font-semibold underline\">FAQ</a> and our <a href=\"/privacy\" class=\"text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 font-semibold underline\">privacy policy</a> for the details of what we store.</p>\n<h2>Contact</h2>\n<p>Questions or problems with the service can be sent through the <a href=\"/contact\" class=\"text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 font-semibold underline\">contact page</a>.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Is a .xyz domain a disposable email domain?</h3>\n<p>Not by itself. .xyz is an ordinary top-level domain used by many legitimate businesses. Some temporary email services happen to use .xyz domains, and many random .xyz domains are cheap enough to be used for throwaway mail, but the extension alone tells you nothing. Websites that block disposable addresses rely on lists of known domains, not on the extension.</p>\n<h3>How can I check whether an email domain accepts real mail?</h3>\n<p>Use our <a href=\"/domain-checker\" class=\"text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 font-semibold underline\">email DNS and MX lookup</a>. If a domain has MX records, it is set up to receive mail; the records also show which provider hosts it. That tells you the domain can receive email, not whether it is disposable or trustworthy.</p>\n<h3>Is DisposeMail the same as dispose.lol, tempmail.xyz or Dispostable?</h3>\n<p>No. These are independent services. DisposeMail is not affiliated with any of them, and we make no claims about how they work.</p>\n<h3>Which spelling is correct?</h3>\n<p>DisposeMail, one word. Searching for dispomail, disposmail or disposalmail will usually still find us, but the address you should see in your browser is <strong>disposemail.xyz</strong>.</p>"
